  "text_md": "![](<2503.07281_pg12_images/imageFile22.png>)\n- Question 2. The second question delves deeper into the subject and it is related to another interesting problem. In analogy with the holomorphic case, we introduce a subspace of the real variable Hardy space H1(R). For b ∈ BMO(R) we consider the pre-annihilator of {b}, that is,\n![](<2503.07281_pg12_images/imageFile1.png>)\n![](<2503.07281_pg12_images/imageFile16.png>)\nNext, suppose (a) holds. Then we have that if f ∈ HΘ1 , then (Θf)|R ∈ H1(R). Indeed, we wish to show that (Θf)|R and H((Θf)|R) ∈ L1(R). Clearly Θf ∈ L1(R), and\n![](<2503.07281_pg12_images/imageFile6.png>)\n- Question 1. Clearly, the ﬁrst question we are interested in is whether there are inner functions Θ, other than the meromorphic inner functions eiτ(·), such that the associated Toeplitz operator TΘ: HΘ1 → H1(C+) is bounded, or equivalently, for which inner functions Θ the equality\nthat is, if and only if the quotient HΘ1 /(KΘ1 ⊕ΘH1) is non-trivial. When non-trivial, it would be interesting to describe it.\n[Θ,H](f1) = ΘH(f1) − H(Θf1) = Θ(−if1) + iΘf1 = 0. Moreover,\nON TOEPLITZ OPERATORS ON H1(C+) 12\n![](<2503.07281_pg12_images/imageFile28.png>)\n![](<2503.07281_pg12_images/imageFile11.png>)\n![](<2503.07281_pg12_images/imageFile5.png>)\nHΘ1 = KΘ1 ⊕ ΘH1\nProof. We note that for every HΘ1 (R) ∋ f = f1 + f2, with f1 ∈ H1(C+)|\nR\n![](<2503.07281_pg12_images/imageFile10.png>)\nand f2 ∈ HΘ1 , by using (1.2) we have that\n![](<2503.07281_pg12_images/imageFile18.png>)\n![](<2503.07281_pg12_images/imageFile23.png>)\n![](<2503.07281_pg12_images/imageFile4.png>)\n![](<2503.07281_pg12_images/imageFile9.png>)\n![](<2503.07281_pg12_images/imageFile31.png>)\n![](<2503.07281_pg12_images/imageFile2.png>)\n- (b) the commutator [Θ,H]: HΘ1 (R) → L1(R) is bounded.\n![](<2503.07281_pg12_images/imageFile24.png>)\n![](<2503.07281_pg12_images/imageFile30.png>)\nR",
